The fixture, the drift, and the check that catches it.

```js
// The stand-in, written in March.
const refundResponse = {
  id: 'rf_123',
  status: 'succeeded',
  amount_cents: 2500,
  created: 1710000000,
};
```

In September the provider ships a change: `amount_cents` becomes `amount`, an object with `value` and `currency`. Every Ashgrove test still passes, because every Ashgrove test asks this object, not the provider. Production starts recording refunds of `undefined`, which becomes `NaN` in a total, which appears on a finance report a fortnight later.

```js
// The contract test, run nightly against the sandbox.
test('refund response still matches our contract', async () => {
  const body = await provider.refunds.create({ amount: 100, currency: 'gbp' });

  // Only what we actually read.
  expect(typeof body.id).toBe('string');
  expect(['succeeded', 'pending', 'failed']).toContain(body.status);
  expect(typeof body.amount_cents).toBe('number');
});
```

This fails in September, names the field, and does so before finance does. Note what it does *not* do: it does not snapshot the whole response. A full-body assertion would fail every time the provider adds an unrelated field, and a test that cries wolf monthly gets muted, which leaves you exactly where you started.

The same contract, applied at the runtime boundary, turns the drift into a clear failure instead of a silent one:

```js
const refund = refundSchema.parse(await provider.refunds.create(input));
```

Now the September change raises 'expected number at amount_cents' at the edge, naming the field, rather than producing `NaN` three layers away.

solution.code:
function contractViolations(response, contract) {
  const violations = [];
  for (const field of Object.keys(contract)) {
    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(response, field)) {
      violations.push('missing:' + field);
    } else if (typeof response[field] !== contract[field]) {
      violations.push('type:' + field);
    }
  }
  violations.sort();
  return violations;
}

