A test double that can only always-fail, and one that can tell a story.

```js
// Always fails: exercises the first attempt and nothing else.
const provider = { charge: async () => { throw new TimeoutError(); } };
```

With this, a retry loop is only ever observed exhausting itself. The case that matters most - the transient failure that succeeds on the third attempt, leaving exactly one charge - cannot be expressed at all.

```js
// Scripted: 'time out twice, then succeed'.
function scriptedProvider(script) {
  let call = 0;
  return {
    charge: async () => {
      const outcome = script[Math.min(call, script.length - 1)];
      call += 1;
      if (outcome === 'timeout') throw new TimeoutError();
      if (outcome === 'malformed') return { unexpected: true };
      return { id: 'ch_1', status: 'succeeded' };
    },
  };
}
```

Now a test can say `scriptedProvider(['timeout', 'timeout', 'ok'])` and assert the thing worth asserting:

```js
await submitOrder(deps, order);
expect(await charges.count()).toBe(1);
```

One charge. Not 'retry was called three times' - a system that retried three times and created three charges would satisfy that assertion perfectly, and it is the bug the test exists to catch.

Repeating the last outcome once the script runs out is a small decision that removes a lot of friction: a test that only cares about a permanent failure writes `['timeout']` rather than counting how many attempts the retry policy will make, so the test does not have to be edited when the policy changes from three attempts to five.

